Yes, you can use Codex for free, but only a little. As of November 26, 2026, OpenAI says Codex is included in every ChatGPT plan, Free and Go included, and its pricing page shows the Free plan getting Codex in the desktop app with the GPT-6 Luna model, "subject to rollout". There is no published message count for Free or Go, Codex cloud tasks are not included on either plan, and the real working allowance starts at Plus. The open-source Codex CLI is free to install, but the model usage behind it is paid for either through a ChatGPT plan or through your own OpenAI API key.

If you typed "codex ai", "codex ai pricing", "codex ia" or "codex von openai" into a search box, this guide is the plain-English version: what Codex is, which of its entry points cost money, what the limits look like by plan, and how it compares with the free use of Claude Code and Gemini CLI. We checked OpenAI's Codex pricing page, its help center, the API pricing page and the GitHub repositories today, and we flag every claim we could not confirm.

{% youtube id="oBvuhcdSsuw" title="Learn 99% of Codex in One Video" /%}

*A third-party walkthrough from the Tech With Tim channel, which says it was recorded on November 3, 2026. We confirmed the title and channel through YouTube oEmbed but did not watch it end to end. Its search transcript says nearly everything can be tried on the free plan but you will use up the allowance fast, which matches OpenAI's own pages. Check any price it quotes against OpenAI's pricing page, because plans change often.*

## Short answer: what is free, what is not

- **Best free way to try Codex:** a ChatGPT Free account with the Codex desktop app. Small unpublished allowance, GPT-6 Luna, no cloud tasks, no image generation.
- **Best first paid step:** ChatGPT Plus at $20 a month. OpenAI's pricing page frames it as enough for "a few focused coding sessions each week" and it unlocks the CLI, IDE extension, web, iOS and cloud tasks.
- **Best for all-day agent work:** ChatGPT Pro, which OpenAI's pricing page now lists at $100, $200 and $500 a month, with no five-hour limit on any of the three.
- **Best open source option:** the Codex CLI (Apache-2.0 on GitHub, 136,568 stars as of November 26, 2026). The client is free, the model calls are not.
- **Best pay-as-you-go route:** Codex with an API key, billed at OpenAI's per-token API rates, no ChatGPT plan needed but no cloud features either.
- **Best free alternative for terminal agents:** Antigravity CLI from Google, whose Individuals plan is $0 with a weekly-refreshed baseline quota. Gemini CLI itself no longer has a free individual tier.
- **Hardest to use for free:** Claude Code. Anthropic's Free plan does not include it, so the cheapest entry is Claude Pro at $20 a month ($17 monthly-equivalent if you pay $200 up front for a year).

{% callout type="info" %}
Quick answer for the impatient: Codex is not a separate subscription. It rides on your ChatGPT plan (Free $0, Go from $8 in the US, Plus $20, Pro $100 to $500) or on API billing. Free and Go can try it. Plus is where most people start using it for real work. All prices are from OpenAI's pages as of November 26, 2026, exclude tax, and can change without notice.
{% /callout %}

One change to know about before you pick a plan: OpenAI's help center says that starting in December 2026, usage allowances on Plus, Pro and Business will be shared across ChatGPT and Codex, and The Verge reported the same on November 25, 2026. We cover what that does and does not tell us in the limits section.

## What Codex is, and why the names are confusing

"Codex" now refers to a family of surfaces from OpenAI rather than a single product. OpenAI's help center describes it as "OpenAI's coding agent that helps you write, review, and ship code", and it is reachable through four clients that all sign in with a ChatGPT account: the ChatGPT desktop app (Codex mode), the Codex CLI, the Codex IDE extension and Codex on the web. A fifth route, Codex cloud, runs tasks on OpenAI-managed machines instead of your laptop.

That is where the "ai agent codex" searches come from. Codex reads a repository, edits files, runs commands and tests, and reports back, with approval settings that control how much it may do without asking. If you only know the word from older OpenAI coding models, the current thing is an agent product with its own apps, its own CLI and its own usage meter.

Three naming traps to keep straight:

1. **Codex is not billed on its own.** There is no "Codex plan". The pricing page says Codex is included in your ChatGPT Free, Go, Plus, Pro, Business, Edu or Enterprise plan, and that those plans share pricing, credits and usage limits with it.
2. **"Free" has three meanings.** A free ChatGPT account (a small allowance), a free open-source client (the CLI, which still needs a model behind it) and a free trial (not offered as a standing trial by OpenAI that we could find).
3. **Model names change fast.** The pricing page today lists GPT-6.2 Astra, GPT-6.2 Sol, GPT-6.1 Sol, GPT-6.1 Terra, GPT-6 Luna and older GPT-5.6 models. The Codex changelog says CLI 0.171.0 on November 20, 2026 added GPT-6.2 Sol and Astra to the model catalogs and made Sol the default. Expect these to rotate again.

There is also a version caveat. Older videos and some blog posts say Codex needs a paid plan, or that the free window was a two-month promotion. One third-party video from a June 2026 round-up even warns that the free and $8 plans cannot use Codex. OpenAI's current help center says the opposite, so we trust the vendor pages dated today, and we recommend you do the same when you read any older guide.

