## Codex in ChatGPT Free: a taste, not a workflow

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/codex-free.png" name="Codex Free" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** The $0 ChatGPT plan includes Codex. OpenAI's pricing page describes the Free tier as a way to "explore Codex capabilities on quick coding tasks", with GPT-6 Luna at Standard speed in the desktop app, subject to rollout. OpenAI's help center confirms Codex is "included across ChatGPT plans, including Free and Go".

**Who it is for.** People who want to see what an agent does with a small repository before paying, students trying a first agentic workflow, and anyone comparing tools.

**Key features.**
- Sign in with a ChatGPT account (a separate Codex signup is not needed).
- Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app (Codex mode).
- Auto-review safety checks are free and do not count toward limits when you are signed in with ChatGPT, per the help center.

**Pros**
- Zero cost to try, and no separate Codex signup.
- Uses the same account you may already have.
- Safety auto-review checks are not metered.

**Cons**
- No published message count for Free. The usage table on the pricing page has columns for Plus, Standard Business and API key only, so we cannot give you a number.
- Codex cloud is explicitly not included with Free or Go.
- Image generation is not available on Free, per the pricing page.
- The pricing page says availability is subject to rollout, so you may not see the same models as another account.

**Benefits for your use case.** If you are asking "is Codex free" because you want to decide whether to pay, Free answers it. You can see how the agent plans, edits files and asks for approval on a small task without spending anything.

**Pricing.** $0. **Free tier:** this is it. **Limits:** unpublished; the in-app usage view and the CLI `/status` command show what you have left once you are signed in.

**Standout use case.** A quick refactor or bug explanation on a small project you already have open locally.

**Skip it if** you plan to run Codex daily, need the CLI or IDE extension guaranteed, or want cloud tasks. We could not confirm from OpenAI's pages whether the Free plan can sign in from the CLI (the pricing page names the desktop app for Free, while the help center lists the CLI as a client for ChatGPT plans generally), so test before you build a workflow on it.

## Codex in ChatGPT Go: lightweight tasks for a low price

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/codex-go.png" name="Codex Go" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** ChatGPT Go is OpenAI's low-cost tier. The January 16, 2026 launch post priced it at $8 a month in the US, and OpenAI's help center says Go prices are shown on the pricing page and vary by region. The Codex pricing page describes Go as "use Codex for lightweight coding tasks", with GPT-6 Luna at Standard speed in the desktop app, subject to rollout.

**Who it is for.** Hobbyists who already use ChatGPT daily for chat and want some Codex on the side.

**Key features.**
- Same access pattern as Free, with a higher allowance for ChatGPT itself (the help center lists Go as having unlimited everyday text chats, like Free, plus extra capacity for tools).
- No annual billing for Go, according to the Go help article.

**Pros**
- Cheapest paid ChatGPT tier.
- Included in the same Codex story as Free, so no new accounts.

**Cons**
- Codex cloud is not included with Go.
- No published Codex message numbers for Go either.
- We could not retrieve a dollar figure for Go from chatgpt.com/pricing today, because the page did not render prices in our fetch. The $8 figure comes from OpenAI's January 2026 launch post and may differ by country or time.

**Benefits for your use case.** If Free runs out quickly for you but a $20 plan feels like too much, Go is the step between them. Whether it gives you materially more Codex than Free is not documented in numbers, so treat it as a test, not a guarantee.

**Pricing.** $8 per month in the US per OpenAI's launch post (January 16, 2026). **Free tier:** none for Go itself, the Free plan is the free route. **Limits:** unpublished.

**Standout use case.** Light daily help in the desktop app while you keep your main work in an editor.

**Skip it if** you need cloud tasks, the IDE extension or predictable numbers. Go straight to Plus, where OpenAI documents ranges.

## Codex in ChatGPT Plus: where most people start working

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/codex-plus.png" name="Codex Plus" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** ChatGPT Plus is $20 a month per OpenAI's help center. For Codex, OpenAI's pricing page says Plus is built to "power a few focused coding sessions each week" and lists Codex on web, in the CLI, in the IDE extension and on iOS, plus cloud integrations such as automatic code review and Slack, access to GPT-6.1 Sol and GPT-6 Luna (and other models in the table), and the option to extend usage with ChatGPT credits.

**Who it is for.** Individual developers who use Codex a few times a week for features, reviews and bug hunts, not for eight-hour autonomous runs.

**Key features.**
- Documented local-message ranges per five-hour window, by model.
- Cloud tasks (Codex cloud is available to eligible Plus accounts, subject to rollout and workspace settings).
- Credits you can buy when you hit a limit; eligible Plus and Pro users can buy credits without changing plans.
- All clients: desktop app, CLI, IDE extension, web.

**Local messages per five hours on Plus (as of November 26, 2026, from OpenAI's Codex pricing page).** OpenAI describes these as estimates rather than fixed limits, and says weekly limits may also apply.

| Model | Messages per 5 hours (Plus and Standard Business) |
| --- | --- |
| GPT-6.2 Astra | 6-50 |
| GPT-6.2 Sol | 17-180 |
| GPT-6.1 Sol | 15-160 |
| GPT-6.1 Terra | 35-285 |
| GPT-6 Astra | 5-45 |
| GPT-6 Sol | 15-150 |
| GPT-6 Luna | 350-3,000 |

The spread inside each row is not a bug. OpenAI says usage depends on the model, where the task runs, task complexity, context, reasoning, speed and tools, and that a long-running task can use far more than a short request. In practice that means the top of a range is for small, simple prompts and the bottom is for large-context agent runs, so plan around the low end.

**Pros**
- First plan with published numbers, so you can reason about it.
- Cloud tasks and every client available.
- Credits as a pressure valve.

**Cons**
- Real work on the strongest models eats the allowance quickly. The Astra range is 6-50 messages per five hours, the lowest in the table.
- Weekly limits may apply on top of the five-hour window.
- A third-party tracker (cg-method.com, read on November 26, 2026) reports the Plus five-hour window was restored in late August 2026 after being removed in July, which shows how often the rules move.

**Benefits for your use case.** Plus is the cheapest plan where Codex is a normal tool rather than a demo. The model dropdown is your throttle: Luna for routine edits stretches the allowance a lot further than Astra or Sol.

**Pricing.** $20 per month. **Free tier:** none; the Free plan is the free route. **Limits:** see the table above; check `/status` in the CLI or the usage dashboard for your actual balance.

**Standout use case.** A weekend of focused feature work with the CLI, switching to a cheaper model for boilerplate and a strong model only for design questions.

**Skip it if** you routinely hit limits mid-session (go to Pro or use API billing) or you only need occasional help (Free or Go may be enough).

## Codex in ChatGPT Pro: $100, $200 and $500 tiers

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/codex-pro.png" name="Codex Pro" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Pro on the Codex pricing page is now three plans, at $100, $200 or $500 USD a month, with Ultrafast mode listed on the $500 tier. The page states "Pro plans currently have no five-hour limit". When ChatGPT Pro launched, OpenAI's January 2026 post listed a single $200 price, so the tier structure is newer than much of what is written online.

**Who it is for.** Developers who keep agents running most of the day, teams of one that treat Codex as the main engineer, and people who were hitting Plus limits repeatedly.

**Key features.**
- No five-hour limit currently, weekly limits still possible.
- Higher included usage on the higher tiers.
- GPT-6 Astra is included within the existing Codex allowance on the $100 and $200 Pro plans, according to the FAQ text on OpenAI's chatgpt.com/codex/pricing page (seen in a search result on November 26, 2026), which adds that Plus and the $20 Business plan include limited Astra usage with the option to buy credits.
- Fast and Ultrafast modes that spend allowance faster for lower latency (the pricing page lists the multipliers per model).

**Pros**
- Removes the five-hour wall, which is the limit people complain about most.
- Three price points instead of a jump from $20 to $200.

**Cons**
- $100 is five times Plus, and it is not obvious from OpenAI's pages exactly how much more Codex it buys beyond "higher".
- OpenAI changes allowances, resets and tiers often. The X posts embedded in this guide show resets, banked resets and a usage-accounting problem within the last six months, so today's numbers may not hold next quarter.
- OpenAI's own pages disagree with older numbers, so any $200-only comparison is stale.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your work has a daily rhythm (you open Codex in the morning and leave it running), the missing five-hour limit changes the shape of your day more than the dollar figure does.

**Pricing.** $100, $200 or $500 per month (as of November 26, 2026, from developers.openai.com/codex/pricing). **Free tier:** none. **Limits:** weekly limits may apply, per OpenAI.

**Standout use case.** Long agent runs on a large codebase with the CLI or desktop app, no hourly babysitting.

**Skip it if** your usage fits inside Plus, or if you would rather pay per token and cap spend yourself.

