## Codex CLI: free software, metered model

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/codex-cli.png" name="Codex CLI" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** The terminal client for Codex, open source under Apache-2.0 at github.com/openai/codex. GitHub's API reports 136,568 stars as of November 26, 2026, a push the same day, and release rust-v0.172.0 on November 24, 2026. The Codex changelog lists CLI 0.172.0 on November 24 and 0.171.0 on November 20.

**Who it is for.** Terminal-first developers, people scripting agents in CI, and anyone who wants to read the client source.

**Key features.**
- Run `codex` in a project folder and choose "Sign in with ChatGPT".
- Slash commands per the CLI docs include `/init`, `/status`, `/permissions`, `/model` and `/review`.
- Non-interactive runs with `codex exec`, resume with `codex resume`, MCP configuration with `codex mcp`, and `codex cloud` for cloud tasks.
- Two billing paths: ChatGPT sign-in (counts against your plan) or an API key (pay per token, no cloud features).

Install options as documented by OpenAI:

Installer script from the Codex CLI docs:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://chatgpt.com/codex/install.sh -o install.sh
sh install.sh
```

Or install with npm (the changelog pins release numbers this way), then start it inside your repo:

```bash
npm install -g @openai/codex
codex
```

Inside a session, type `/status` to see your remaining allowance.

**Pros**
- The client costs nothing and its source is public.
- One tool for ChatGPT-plan use and API-key use.
- `/status` tells you where you stand.

**Cons**
- It is a client, not a free model. Without a ChatGPT plan that includes it or an API key, it does not do work.
- Whether the Free plan can use the CLI is not spelled out on the pricing page (see the Free section).
- It changes quickly, with a new release roughly every few days.

**Benefits for your use case.** If you live in a terminal, the CLI is the most direct way to see exactly what you are paying for, because `/status` and the usage dashboard show the same pool.

**Pricing.** Free software. Model use is billed through your ChatGPT plan or API key. **Free tier:** whatever your ChatGPT plan includes. **Limits:** your plan's allowance, or none beyond API rate limits when you use a key.

**Standout use case.** `codex exec` in a script for repeatable code review or migration chores.

**Skip it if** you prefer a GUI (use the desktop app) or you want the model free too (see Antigravity below).

## Codex cloud: tasks on OpenAI's machines

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/codex-cloud.png" name="Codex cloud" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Codex cloud runs coding tasks on OpenAI-managed computers using reusable cloud environments. OpenAI's help center says you create and publish an environment on desktop or web, then start and continue tasks from desktop, web or mobile, and that cloud tasks can keep working while your computer is asleep.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want parallel background tasks, teams that need work to continue off their laptops, and anyone who dislikes leaving a machine on.

**Key features.**
- Reusable environments you set up once.
- Task continuation while your device sleeps.
- Auto code review and Slack-style integrations listed on the Plus plan.

**Pros**
- Frees your machine and lets work run in parallel.
- At launch, standard environments carry no separate VM charge; only model usage counts.

**Cons**
- Not included with Free or Go.
- Eligibility is "subject to rollout and workspace settings", and guest, K-12 and Enterprise view-only seats cannot create cloud environments, per the help center.
- Cloud tasks can use more of your allowance than local ones (a third-party source says so; OpenAI says only that usage depends on where the task runs).
- Cloud access is off by default for Enterprise workspaces that have not enabled it.

**Benefits for your use case.** It turns a small allowance into a queue of background jobs, but it also spends that allowance without you watching, so keep an eye on `/status` or the dashboard.

**Pricing.** No separate VM charge at launch; model usage counts toward your plan's limits and applicable credits (OpenAI help center, as of November 26, 2026). **Free tier:** none. **Limits:** shared with your plan.

**Standout use case.** Kick off a refactor before lunch and come back to a diff.

**Skip it if** you are on Free or Go, or you do not want code leaving your machine. Check OpenAI's data controls and your own policies first.

## Codex with an API key: pay per token

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/codex-api-key.png" name="Codex API key" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** The CLI, SDK and IDE extension can run on an OpenAI API key instead of a ChatGPT plan. The Codex pricing page says this mode has no cloud features (no GitHub code review or Slack), model availability follows the API, and you pay API pricing. The help center also says the August 31, 2026 removal of GPT-5.4 and GPT-5.4 mini from ChatGPT-signed-in Codex does not affect API-key use.

**Who it is for.** People who want a hard spending cap, automation in CI, or who would rather pay for exactly what they use.

**API prices (Standard tier, per 1M tokens, as of November 26, 2026, from OpenAI's API pricing page).**

| Model | Input | Cached input | Output |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| gpt-6.2-astra | $8.00 | $0.40 | $40.00 |
| gpt-6.2-sol | $2.00 | $0.10 | $10.00 |
| gpt-6.1-sol | $2.00 | $0.10 | $10.00 |
| gpt-6.1-terra | $1.00 | $0.05 | $5.00 |
| gpt-6-luna | $0.10 | $0.01 | $0.50 |
| gpt-5.3-codex | $3.50 | $0.35 | $28.00 |

The Batch tier is half of Standard for these rows, and Fast mode costs more; check the page before budgeting.

Illustrative arithmetic (ours, not OpenAI's): a run that reads 1M uncached input tokens and writes 100K output tokens costs $3.00 on GPT-6.2 Sol ($2.00 plus $1.00), $12.00 on GPT-6.2 Astra, and $0.15 on GPT-6 Luna. Real tasks cache part of the input, so treat these as ceilings for simple cases.

OpenAI's pricing page also shows credit rates for ChatGPT-plan users who go over their allowance, per 1M tokens in credits: GPT-6.2 Astra 200 input, 10 cached, 1,000 output; GPT-6.2 Sol and GPT-6.1 Sol 50, 2.5 and 250; GPT-6.1 Terra 25, 1.25 and 125; GPT-6 Luna 2.5, 0.25 and 12.5. Comparing the credit and dollar rows implies 25 credits per dollar of API-equivalent usage, but that is our inference, not a price list for buying credits.

**Pros**
- No plan caps and a clear price per token.
- Works for automation and CI.

**Cons**
- No cloud features in this mode.
- Easy to overspend on a strong model with long contexts.
- You manage keys and billing yourself.

**Benefits for your use case.** The pricing page notes that when you hit a plan limit, all users can run extra local chats with an API key. That makes API billing a clean overflow for a Plus plan.

**Pricing.** Per token as above. **Free tier:** none. **Limits:** API rate limits, no plan message caps.

**Standout use case.** Overflow when a Plus limit hits mid-task, or a metered CI job.

**Skip it if** you want a flat monthly price or need the cloud features.

