## Claude Code: the closest rival, with no free plan

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/claude-code.png" name="Claude Code" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Anthropic's coding agent for the terminal and other surfaces. Its docs list install paths through a shell script, a Homebrew cask, a Windows PowerShell script and npm. GitHub's API reports 157,195 stars for anthropics/claude-code as of November 26, 2026 and shows no license on the repository, so we do not call it open source.

**Who it is for.** Developers already on Claude who want an agent in the terminal, and teams comparing Anthropic and OpenAI agents.

**Key features.**
- Included with Claude Pro, Max and Ultra according to Anthropic's pricing page; the Free plan does not include it.
- Shares one 5-hour and weekly usage window with the rest of Claude.
- Skills, subagents and hooks, which is why many entries in our catalog target it.

Install with npm, as listed in Anthropic's docs:

```bash
npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code
```

**Pros**
- Mature skills and subagent ecosystem.
- Clear plan ladder on Anthropic's page.

**Cons**
- No free route. The Free plan on claude.com/pricing lists Claude Code as not included.
- Anthropic does not publish message counts, only "more usage than Free every 5 hours and every week", so comparing it with Codex's published ranges is apples to oranges.
- Closed-source client.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your question is "which agent can I try without paying", Claude Code loses the first round. If you already pay for Claude Pro, it is included.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, claude.com/pricing).** Pro $20 per month, or $17 per month billed annually ($200 up front). Max from $100 (5x Pro usage). Ultra $200 (10x Pro weekly usage and $200 a month in API credits, per Anthropic's page). **Free tier:** none for Claude Code. **Limits:** 5-hour and weekly windows.

**Standout use case.** Large-context refactors with skills and subagents you already own.

**Skip it if** you want a free agent, or you do not use Claude.

For the Claude side in more depth, see our [Is Claude free guide](https://agentskills.codes/blog/is-claude-free) and the sister-site comparison of [Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and Copilot CLI](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/claude-code-vs-codex-vs-gemini-cli-vs-copilot-cli-2026).

## Gemini CLI: no longer the free one

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/gemini-cli.png" name="Gemini CLI" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Google's open-source terminal agent (Apache-2.0; 109,185 GitHub stars as of November 26, 2026). Older videos and posts still describe a generous free tier with a Gemini 2.5 Pro allowance. That is out of date. The repository README now states that free individual access and Google AI Pro and Ultra access "moved to Antigravity CLI on June 18, 2026", and that Gemini CLI is for organizations with Gemini Code Assist Standard or Enterprise licenses (plus legacy paid API or Vertex keys).

**Who it is for.** Teams already on Gemini Code Assist and Google Cloud.

**Key features.**
- Open source, `gemini` command.
- The README says it is not tuned for the Gemini 4 series and should not be used to benchmark Gemini models.

**Pros**
- Open source with a large community.
- Fits Google Cloud shops.

**Cons**
- No free tier today, which reverses what many older guides say.
- Positioned as an enterprise tool, with newer models routed to Antigravity CLI.

**Benefits for your use case.** Knowing this saves you from a stale tutorial: if you want a free Google agent in 2026, install Antigravity CLI, not Gemini CLI.

**Pricing.** Depends on your Code Assist or API arrangement; we found no public per-seat price on the pages we fetched. **Free tier:** none. **Limits:** set by your license.

**Standout use case.** An enterprise already paying for Code Assist that wants a terminal agent.

**Skip it if** you are an individual looking for free use.

## Antigravity CLI: the free Google route

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-codex-free-pricing-and-limits/logos/antigravity-cli.png" name="Antigravity CLI" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Google's current multi-agent coding terminal (`agy`), part of Google Antigravity, which also ships an app, IDE extensions and a Python SDK. Google's Antigravity pricing page lists an Individuals plan at $0 per month with access across the app, CLI, IDE extensions and Python SDK, and a "baseline agent quota refreshed weekly".

**Who it is for.** Individual developers who want a free agent.

**Key features.**
- The $0 plan lists Gemini 4 Hydrogen, Gemini 3.8 Flash and, on that page, Claude Sonnet 4.6 (thinking), Opus 4.6 and GPT-OSS-120b, with a note that some models are removed on November 30, 2026.
- Google AI Pro gets quotas refreshed every five hours and Ultra gets the highest. Enterprise starts from $30 per seat per month through Gemini Enterprise, per the page.

**Pros**
- The only one of these three vendors with a free individual coding-agent plan today.
- Several model families on the free page.

**Cons**
- A weekly refresh means a burst of work can leave you waiting days.
- The quota is called "baseline" and no numbers are published.
- Model availability is changing (the November 30, 2026 removals).
- We did not run it and cannot speak to output quality.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your budget is zero and you need more than a trial, this is the practical alternative to Codex Free.

**Pricing.** Individuals $0 per month. AI Pro and Ultra prices are not shown on the Antigravity page, so we leave them out. **Free tier:** yes. **Limits:** baseline quota refreshed weekly.

**Standout use case.** Weekly side-project work with no card on file.

**Skip it if** you need a published message budget or a model that is not on the free page.

{% figure src="https://antigravity.google/og/index.png" alt="Google Antigravity site social card" caption="Google Antigravity's site social card. Source: Google (antigravity.google)" /%}

## How Codex limits work (and how to stretch them)

OpenAI's help center and pricing page describe a handful of rules worth knowing before you spend money.

**Two kinds of windows.** Usage is metered over a five-hour period and, on some plans, weekly. The pricing page says "Pro plans currently have no five-hour limit" and that weekly limits may also apply. A third-party tracker reports the Plus five-hour window was removed in July 2026 and restored in late August; we could not confirm those dates on OpenAI's pages, so treat them as reported, not verified.

**One shared pool.** The help center says Codex, ChatGPT Work, ChatGPT for Excel and workspace agents share an allowance and credit pool where those features exist on your plan.

**Where it is heading.** The help center article "Understanding ChatGPT and Codex usage limits" (shown as updated 2 days ago when we read it) says that starting in December 2026, Plus, Pro and Business allowances will be shared across ChatGPT and Codex, measured in tokens, with instant chat not drawing from usage. OpenAI says it expects "most users won't need to wait for their usage to reset more often than they do now", and The Verge reported the plan on November 25, 2026. OpenAI has not published how large the combined allowances will be, so we cannot tell you whether your Plus plan will feel bigger or smaller.

**What you see.** Settings or the usage dashboard shows which allowance is exhausted, your credit balance and any reset time. In a CLI session, type `/status`.

**What happens at the limit.** An active turn can continue under fair use, Plus and Pro users can buy credits, and everyone can run extra local chats with an API key. Support does not reset limits for you. OpenAI says occasional one-time resets happen but are not guaranteed.

**Banked resets.** OpenAI has been rolling out rate-limit banking, where you save a reset and use it when you need it. The OpenAIDevs post above says an invited friend's first Codex message adds one to the bank for both of you, rolling out to Go, Plus, Pro and Business as of June 12, 2026. We did not test it. For the full mechanics, see our dedicated guide to [Codex usage limits for agent workflows](https://agentskills.codes/blog/codex-usage-limits-agent-workflows) and AgentPedia's [Codex out-of-usage resets guide](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/codex-out-of-usage-resets-guide).

{% x id="2075641131002700120" /%}

*Thibault Sottiaux (@thsottiaux), July 10, 2026, announcing a usage reset across Codex and ChatGPT Work. Confirmed through X syndication on November 26, 2026.*

**Ways to stretch an allowance (from OpenAI's help center and pricing page):**

1. Pick a cheaper model for routine work. On Plus, GPT-6 Luna allows 350-3,000 messages per five hours against 5-45 for GPT-6 Astra.
2. Keep your `AGENTS.md` small. OpenAI lists a smaller AGENTS.md as a way to reduce usage.
3. Turn off MCP servers you are not using. OpenAI lists fewer MCP servers as a usage reducer too.
4. Avoid Fast and Ultrafast modes unless latency matters. OpenAI's page says they spend included usage at a higher rate (2x to 2.5x for Fast, 6x to 8x for Ultrafast, depending on the model).
5. Run `/status` before you kick off a long cloud task.

One more item to price in: voice in the desktop app costs $0.05 per minute where flexible pricing applies, per the pricing page, and building or editing a Site counts toward Codex usage.

