## Head-to-head: which to pick

### Codex Free vs Antigravity CLI Individuals: which free plan to pick

Both are $0. Codex Free gives you the desktop app with GPT-6 Luna and no published allowance. Antigravity Individuals gives a baseline weekly-refreshed quota across app, CLI, IDE extensions and SDK with a wider listed model set. **Pick Codex Free** if you want OpenAI's agent in the ChatGPT desktop app and are happy with small tasks. **Pick Antigravity** if you want the terminal and IDE surfaces for free and can live with a weekly refresh. Neither publishes message numbers, so run both on one real task before choosing.

### Codex Plus vs Claude Pro: which $20 plan to pick

Both are $20 a month ($17 annual-equivalent for Claude). Codex Plus gives published per-model ranges (for example 15-160 messages per five hours on GPT-6.1 Sol) and cloud tasks. Claude Pro includes Claude Code but gives no message counts. Third-party video reviewers we found in search results reach opposite conclusions on which gives more headroom, and we ran no benchmark, so we cannot settle it. **Pick Codex Plus** if you want published numbers and an agent inside a ChatGPT subscription you may already have. **Pick Claude Pro** if you prefer Claude models and its skills and subagent ecosystem.

### Codex CLI with ChatGPT sign-in vs Codex CLI with an API key: which billing to pick

Same client, different meter. A ChatGPT sign-in draws on a flat-priced allowance and unlocks cloud features. An API key charges per token with no cloud features and no plan caps. **Pick ChatGPT sign-in** for steady daily use under your allowance. **Pick an API key** for CI, spend-capped automation or overflow when Plus runs out.

## Skills and MCP servers that work with these tools

Codex reads `AGENTS.md`, and the CLI can configure MCP servers with `codex mcp`. Our catalog has real entries for working with it (each page below returned HTTP 200 on November 26, 2026):

- [codex](https://agentskills.codes/skills/codex): the Lucklyric skill that invokes the Codex CLI for complex coding tasks, handy if you drive Codex from another agent.
- [codex-cli-bridge](https://agentskills.codes/skills/codex-cli-bridge): bridges Claude Code and Codex CLI and generates `AGENTS.md` from `CLAUDE.md`, useful if you run both tools.
- [codex-skill](https://agentskills.codes/skills/codex-skill): lets Claude hand implementation work to Codex.
- [codex-code-review](https://agentskills.codes/skills/codex-code-review): code reviews through the Codex CLI.
- [codex-worker](https://agentskills.codes/skills/codex-worker): spawns multiple Codex CLI agents through tmux, which also multiplies how fast you spend an allowance.
- [codex-review](https://agentskills.codes/skills/codex-review): code review with automatic changelog generation, integrated with Codex.
- [mcp-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder): a guide for building MCP servers, usable with any client that speaks MCP.
- [mcp-integration](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-integration): guidance on adding MCP servers to a plugin or agent setup.

Install a hosted catalog skill with the download endpoint. The id comes from the catalog API (`codex` is id 18):

```bash
curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/18 -o skill.zip \
  && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.claude/skills/codex
```

That path targets Claude Code's skill folder. For how `AGENTS.md` relates to skills and other rule files, see our explainer on [AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md vs SKILL.md vs Cursor rules](https://agentskills.codes/blog/agents-md-vs-claude-md-vs-skill-md-vs-cursor-rules). If you are choosing models inside Codex, read our guide to [GPT-6.1 Sol for agent workloads](https://agentskills.codes/blog/gpt-6-1-sol-agent-workloads) and the [cost-planning guide to Ultrafast and Pro tiers](https://agentskills.codes/blog/ultrafast-and-pro-tiers-cost-planning). For free options beyond these three, see [best free AI coding agents in 2026](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026).

