## Common mistakes

1. **Believing the "free for two months" posts.** Early Codex coverage described a limited promotion. OpenAI's current help center says Codex is included across plans, including Free and Go, and states no end date there.
2. **Assuming "Codex" is one price.** It follows your ChatGPT plan or your API key. Check which meter you are on with `/status`.
3. **Following a Gemini CLI free-tier tutorial.** The repository README says free individual access moved to Antigravity CLI on June 18, 2026.
4. **Planning around the top of a range.** The upper number is for small prompts. A big agent run on a strong model sits near the bottom.
5. **Leaving every MCP server on.** OpenAI lists fewer MCP servers as a way to cut usage.
6. **Treating third-party limit numbers as official.** Trackers and videos are useful, but OpenAI's pages are the source and they change.
7. **Forgetting the December change.** Shared ChatGPT and Codex allowances are coming on Plus, Pro and Business.

## Checklist before you choose a plan

- [ ] Do you only need to see what Codex does? Use Free.
- [ ] Do you need the CLI or IDE extension on Free or Go? Test sign-in first, since OpenAI's pages are not explicit.
- [ ] Do you need cloud tasks? You need Plus or higher.
- [ ] Do you hit limits inside one five-hour session? Look at Pro or API overflow.
- [ ] Do you need a spending cap? Use an API key.
- [ ] Are you a team? Compare Business admin controls.
- [ ] Have you read the current pricing page today, not a cached blog post?

## How we evaluated

We fetched OpenAI's Codex pricing page, Codex CLI and cloud docs, auth and changelog pages, its API pricing page and two help center articles on November 26, 2026. We also read Anthropic's pricing page, the Gemini CLI GitHub README, the Antigravity pricing page and the GitHub repositories, and pulled star counts from the GitHub API the same day. We checked every YouTube and X embed against YouTube oEmbed and the X syndication endpoint, and confirmed each image URL returns HTTP 200 with an image content type.

What we did not do: we did not run Codex, Claude Code, Gemini CLI or Antigravity CLI for this guide, we did not sign in to a Free plan to check the real allowance, we did not benchmark output quality, and we did not buy any plan. The chatgpt.com/pricing page did not render dollar amounts in our fetch, so Plus at $20 comes from OpenAI's help center and Go at $8 from its January 2026 launch post. We take no affiliate fees and have no relationship with any vendor named here.

## FAQ

### Is Codex free?

Partly. OpenAI says Codex is included in ChatGPT Free and Go plans, with the Free plan listed on its pricing page at $0 for quick tasks in the desktop app. The allowance is small, no message count is published for Free, and cloud tasks are not included. The CLI is free to install, but it needs a ChatGPT plan or an API key to do model work.

### Is Codex AI free for students?

We did not find a Codex-specific student discount on OpenAI's pages. Students can use the Free plan like anyone else, and OpenAI lists Education plans for institutions with their own terms. Check with your school.

### How much does Codex cost per month?

There is no Codex price on its own. It uses your ChatGPT plan: Free $0, Go from $8 in the US, Plus $20, Pro $100, $200 or $500, as of November 26, 2026. Business and Enterprise are priced per seat or by agreement. Or you can pay per token with an API key.

### What is the difference between Codex and ChatGPT?

ChatGPT is OpenAI's assistant. Codex is its coding agent, reachable from the ChatGPT desktop app, a CLI, an IDE extension and the web. They share plans and, from December 2026 on Plus, Pro and Business, a combined usage allowance according to OpenAI's help center.

### What does "codex ia" or "codex von openai" mean?

Those are the same Codex in other languages: OpenAI's coding agent ("ia" is the Spanish and French abbreviation for AI, and "von OpenAI" is German for "from OpenAI"). Do not confuse it with the older Codex model OpenAI released years ago, which is a different thing.

### Is the Codex CLI free?

The software is free and open source under Apache-2.0. Using it costs money or plan allowance because requests go to OpenAI models. You sign in with ChatGPT or supply an API key.

### Can I use Codex without a ChatGPT subscription?

Yes, two ways: the Free ChatGPT plan, or an OpenAI API key paid per token. The API-key mode has no cloud features, per OpenAI's pricing page.

### What are Codex's usage limits on Plus?

OpenAI's pricing page gives per-five-hour local message ranges by model, for example 350-3,000 for GPT-6 Luna, 15-160 for GPT-6.1 Sol and 5-45 for GPT-6 Astra, and says they are estimates, not fixed limits. Weekly limits may also apply.

### Does Codex have a five-hour limit?

Plus and Standard Business show five-hour ranges. For Pro, OpenAI's page says "Pro plans currently have no five-hour limit".

### What happens when I run out of Codex usage?

You can wait for the reset time, buy credits on Plus or Pro, use an API key for extra local chats, or upgrade. OpenAI says an active turn can continue under fair use. Support does not reset limits. See our [limits guide](https://agentskills.codes/blog/codex-usage-limits-agent-workflows).

### Is Codex better than Claude Code?

We did not benchmark them and no neutral benchmark exists that we can cite. On cost, Codex has a free entry (the Free plan) and Claude Code does not. Reviewers disagree on quality and limit headroom, so test both on your own repo.

### Is Gemini CLI still free?

No. Its README says free individual access moved to Antigravity CLI on June 18, 2026. Antigravity's Individuals plan is $0 with a weekly-refreshed baseline quota.

### Will Codex limits change in December?

OpenAI says Plus, Pro and Business will share one allowance across ChatGPT and Codex starting in December 2026. It has not published the new sizes.

### Does Codex cloud cost extra?

At launch, OpenAI says standard cloud environments have no separate VM charge, but model usage counts against your limits and credits.

